To mitigate this vulnerability for affected products, remove the use of the none method in the smtp_auth directive in your NGINX configuration or avoid using the Auth-Wait response header in the HTTP authentication server until you can update your product version. To do so, perform the following procedure:

Impact of action: Misconfigured authentication can prevent SMTP access. F5 recommends testing changes during a maintenance window. Additionally, the configuration can be stubbed out to multiple files; be sure to review all appropriate configuration sections and to test changes one at a time.

Log in to the command line of your NGINX host system with administrative privileges.
Change directories to your NGINX configuration directory, usually in /etc/nginx.
Using your preferred text editor, locate all configuration blocks containing smtp_auth directives and remove the none method. For example, remove none from the following block:



server {
  listen 25;
  protocol smtp;
  smtp_auth none;
}

Replace none with an authentication method appropriate for your environment. The following example uses login plain:

server {
  listen 25;
  protocol smtp;
  smtp_auth login plain;
}

Note: The smtp_auth directives are supported in the mail and server contexts. For more information about configuration options, refer to the following NGINX module documents:

The NGINX Module ngx_mail_smtp_module page.
The NGINX Module ngx_mail_core_module page.
Save your changes and exit the text editor.
Test the configuration changes by entering the following command:

sudo nginx -t

The output should appear similar to the following example:

nginx: the configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf syntax is ok
nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful

Reload the NGINX configuration by entering the following command:

sudo service nginx reload

