To mitigate this vulnerability for affected products, you can add a check to your location block to ensure that the HTTP Destination request header is either empty or that it starts with the location name. To do so, perform the following procedure:

Impact of action: F5 recommends that you test these changes during a maintenance window and consider the possible impact on your specific environment.

Log in to the command line of your NGINX host system with administrative privileges.
Change directories to your NGINX configuration directory, usually in /etc/nginx.

Note: The configuration can be stubbed out to multiple files, so be sure to review all appropriate configuration sections.

Using your preferred text editor, find the WebDAV location block in your configuration file. For example:

Note: If there are multiple WebDAV configuration blocks, F5 recommends that you test changes one at a time.

location /foo {
    dav_methods PUT DELETE MKCOL COPY MOVE;
    create_full_put_path on;
    dav_access    group:rw all:r;
    limit_except GET {
        allow 192.168.1.0/32;
        deny all;
    }
}

In the beginning of the WebDAV location block, add an 'if' statement to check that the HTTP Destination request header is either empty or that it starts with the expected location name. If neither case is true, return an HTTP 400 Bad Request error code to the client. For example:

location /foo {
    if ($http_destination !~* '^$|/foo') {
        return 400;
    }
    dav_methods PUT DELETE MKCOL COPY MOVE;
    create_full_put_path on;
    dav_access    group:rw all:r;
    limit_except GET {
        allow 192.168.1.0/32;
        deny all;
    }
}

Save changes and exit the text editor.
Test the configuration changes by entering the following command:

sudo nginx -t

The output should appear similar to the following example:

nginx: the configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf syntax is ok
nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful

Reload the NGINX configuration by entering the following command:

sudo service nginx reload

For more information about configuration options for the ngx_http_dav_module module, refer to the Module ngx_http_dav_module page.

