To mitigate this vulnerability, you can check the $ssl_client_verify variable and reject access to the service as appropriate. For example, in proxying configurations, when $ssl_client_verify is invalid, you can direct traffic to an invalid address. To do so, perform the following procedure:

Impact of action: The following procedure modifies your proxy routing conditions and outcomes. F5 recommends that you test these changes during a maintenance window and consider the possible impact on your specific environment.

Log in to the command line of your NGINX host system with administrative privileges.
Change directories to your NGINX configuration directory, usually in /etc/nginx.

Note: The configuration can be stubbed out to multiple files, so be sure to review all appropriate configuration sections.

Using your preferred text editor, locate all configuration blocks containing the ssl_verify_client on and ssl_ocsp on directives and direct traffic to an invalid address when the $ssl_client_verify variable is invalid. For example:

Note: If there are multiple configuration blocks with ssl_verify_client on and ssl_ocsp on directives, F5 recommends that you test changes one at a time. Additionally, ssl_verify_client on and ssl_ocsp on directives are supported in the http, stream, and server contexts.

stream {
  upstream upstream {
    server 10.0.0.10:443;
  }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    [...]
    # ssl verify client and ssl ocsp are on
    ssl_verify_client on;
    ssl_ocsp on;
    [...]
    proxy_pass upstream;
  }
}


Edit the configuration as needed. In the following example, you add the following configuration in bold to:
define an invalid upstream IP address
use map to set the variable $invalid when $ssl_client_verify fails
direct traffic upstream accordingly

stream {
  upstream upstream {
    server 10.0.0.10:443;
  }
  # Define an invalid IP address 
  upstream upstreaminvalid {
    server 10.0.0.200:443;
  }
  map $ssl_client_verify $invalid {
    ~FAILED "invalid";
  }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    [...]
    # ssl verify client and ssl ocsp are on
    ssl_verify_client on;
    ssl_ocsp on;
    [...]
    proxy_pass upstream$invalid;
  }
}


Save changes and exit the text editor.
Test the configuration changes by entering the following command:

sudo nginx -t

The output should appear similar to the following example:

nginx: the configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf syntax is ok
nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful

Reload the NGINX configuration by entering the following command:

sudo service nginx reload

For more information about configuration options for the ngx_stream_ssl_module module, refer to the Module ngx_stream_ssl_module page.

